WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.047
This paper, Mechanistic Mode Connectivity,

00:00:03.127 --> 00:00:07.862
studies neural network loss landscapes through the
lens of mode connectivity.

00:00:07.942 --> 00:00:13.554
The authors ask whether minimizers that rely on
different mechanisms for making predictions

00:00:13.634 --> 00:00:16.243
are connected via simple paths of low loss.

00:00:16.323 --> 00:00:21.371
They define mechanistic similarity as shared
invariances to input transformations,

00:00:21.451 --> 00:00:27.751
and show that a lack of linear connectivity between
two models implies they use dissimilar mechanisms.

00:00:27.831 --> 00:00:32.128
Building on this insight, they propose
connectivity-based fine-tuning,

00:00:32.208 --> 00:00:38.320
a method to deliberately alter a model's mechanisms
and reduce its reliance on spurious attributes.

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:42.987
Modern deep networks have infinitely many global
minimizers,

00:00:43.067 --> 00:00:47.355
and the mode-connectivity literature shows these
minimizers are joined by surprisingly simple,

00:00:47.435 --> 00:00:51.722
low-loss paths.

00:00:51.802 --> 00:00:56.090
But this literature has ignored a crucial question:
what mechanisms do the connected models actually use?

00:00:56.170 --> 00:01:00.457
Two models can both reach low loss while relying on
entirely different attributes of the input,

00:01:00.537 --> 00:01:04.825
such as an object's background versus its shape.

00:01:04.905 --> 00:01:09.192
The paper argues we cannot understand loss
landscapes, or safely fine-tune models,

00:01:09.273 --> 00:01:13.560
without accounting for these prediction mechanisms.

00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:15.519
Why does this matter now?

00:01:15.599 --> 00:01:20.365
In practice we constantly fine-tune pretrained
models on downstream data,

00:01:20.445 --> 00:01:23.086
assuming this fixes undesirable behavior.

00:01:23.166 --> 00:01:29.790
But prior work shows fine-tuned models often remain
linearly connected to their pretraining solution.

00:01:29.870 --> 00:01:33.241
If linear connectivity is tied to shared mechanisms,

00:01:33.321 --> 00:01:39.348
then naive fine-tuning on clean data might never
remove a model's reliance on spurious cues.

00:01:39.428 --> 00:01:45.122
Understanding the relationship between connectivity
and mechanism is therefore directly

00:01:45.202 --> 00:01:48.640
relevant to robustness and safe adaptation of models.

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:52.822
The paper makes three main contributions.

00:01:52.902 --> 00:01:56.704
First,

00:01:56.784 --> 00:02:00.587
it defines mechanistic similarity: two models are
mechanistically similar if they are

00:02:00.667 --> 00:02:04.469
invariant to the same set of unit interventions on
the data-generating process.

00:02:04.549 --> 00:02:08.351
Second, it characterizes connectivity,

00:02:08.431 --> 00:02:12.233
proving that if two models lack linear connectivity
up to architectural symmetries,

00:02:12.313 --> 00:02:16.116
they must be mechanistically dissimilar.

00:02:16.196 --> 00:02:19.998
Third, motivated by this result, it proposes
Connectivity-Based Fine-Tuning,

00:02:20.078 --> 00:02:23.880
a method that exploits loss barriers to steer a
model toward the mechanisms we actually want.

00:02:24.260 --> 00:02:29.564
The method rests on defining mechanistic similarity
through invariance to interventions

00:02:29.644 --> 00:02:31.483
on the data-generating process.

00:02:31.563 --> 00:02:37.115
The authors then prove that a loss barrier along
the linear path between two models implies

00:02:37.195 --> 00:02:39.714
the models are mechanistically dissimilar,

00:02:39.794 --> 00:02:42.190
they have learned different invariances.

00:02:42.270 --> 00:02:45.903
Connectivity-Based Fine-Tuning turns this into an
algorithm.

00:02:45.983 --> 00:02:50.545
It minimizes three terms: a cross-entropy loss on a
small cue-free dataset,

00:02:50.625 --> 00:02:57.724
a barrier loss that deliberately raises loss along
the linear path to the cue-relying solution up to an upper bound,

00:02:57.804 --> 00:03:04.779
and an invariance loss that aligns
penultimate-layer representations across counterfactual versions of each class.

00:03:04.859 --> 00:03:08.617
Together these push the model to a mechanistically
dissimilar,

00:03:08.697 --> 00:03:10.040
cue-invariant solution.

00:03:10.420 --> 00:03:12.794
To measure mechanisms quantitatively,

00:03:12.874 --> 00:03:19.890
the authors build synthetic datasets by embedding
easily separable spurious cues into standard vision data.

00:03:19.970 --> 00:03:23.802
CIFAR-10 gets small box cues placed according to
the label,

00:03:23.882 --> 00:03:27.715
CIFAR-100 gets cues colored and positioned by label
digits,

00:03:27.795 --> 00:03:33.551
and the Dominoes dataset stacks CIFAR-10 images
with class-matched Fashion-MNIST images.

00:03:33.631 --> 00:03:38.392
They vary the fraction of cued samples from sixty
to one hundred percent,

00:03:38.472 --> 00:03:43.830
and pair every dataset with counterfactual test
sets that remove, keep, randomize,

00:03:43.910 --> 00:03:45.157
or scramble the cue.

00:03:45.237 --> 00:03:52.120
These counterfactuals reveal exactly how much a
model leans on the spurious cue versus the natural image.

00:03:52.500 --> 00:03:55.754
The central empirical finding validates the theory.

00:03:55.834 --> 00:04:00.003
When ResNet-18 models are trained with and without
spurious cues,

00:04:00.083 --> 00:04:02.356
they are mechanistically dissimilar,

00:04:02.436 --> 00:04:06.409
and the paper shows they cannot be connected by a
linear path,

00:04:06.489 --> 00:04:09.612
even after accounting for permutation symmetries.

00:04:09.692 --> 00:04:12.881
However, a quadratic path connects them with ease.

00:04:12.961 --> 00:04:18.437
This confirms that linear disconnection is a
reliable signal of differing mechanisms.

00:04:18.517 --> 00:04:24.124
It also explains why naive fine-tuning fails:
fine-tuned models stay linearly connected

00:04:24.204 --> 00:04:29.550
to their pretraining solution and therefore keep
relying on the same spurious cues.

00:04:29.930 --> 00:04:34.086
The authors ablate the two auxiliary terms in the
CBFT objective.

00:04:34.166 --> 00:04:40.473
Removing the barrier loss prevents the model from
moving to a mechanistically dissimilar solution,

00:04:40.553 --> 00:04:46.338
while removing the invariance loss means the model
no longer reliably selects the specific

00:04:46.418 --> 00:04:48.880
cue-invariant mechanism the user wants.

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:52.203
In other words, the barrier loss handles the where,

00:04:52.283 --> 00:04:55.071
pushing away from the cue-relying minimizer,

00:04:55.151 --> 00:05:00.219
and the invariance loss handles the which, locking
onto the desired invariance.

00:05:00.299 --> 00:05:02.370
Both are needed for CBFT to work.

00:05:02.750 --> 00:05:05.039
The numbers make the effect concrete.

00:05:05.119 --> 00:05:07.664
On CIFAR-10 with sixty percent cued data,

00:05:07.744 --> 00:05:13.169
Connectivity-Based Fine-Tuning achieves
seventy-four percent accuracy without the cue,

00:05:13.249 --> 00:05:15.282
seventy-two percent with the cue,

00:05:15.362 --> 00:05:18.675
and seventy-three percent when the cue is randomized,

00:05:18.755 --> 00:05:20.788
essentially invariant to the cue.

00:05:20.868 --> 00:05:23.668
A standard fine-tuning baseline, by contrast,

00:05:23.748 --> 00:05:30.134
hits ninety-nine percent with the cue but drops to
just seventeen percent when the cue is randomized,

00:05:30.214 --> 00:05:33.271
revealing heavy reliance on the spurious feature.

00:05:33.351 --> 00:05:37.625
And when the underlying image is randomized, CBFT
falls near chance,

00:05:37.705 --> 00:05:42.170
around nine percent, showing it no longer predicts
from the cue at all.

00:05:42.550 --> 00:05:46.815
The takeaway is that mode connectivity carries
mechanistic meaning.

00:05:46.895 --> 00:05:52.328
If two low-loss models are not linearly connected,
they rely on different mechanisms,

00:05:52.408 --> 00:05:54.922
and this fact can be turned into a tool.

00:05:55.002 --> 00:06:00.564
Connectivity-Based Fine-Tuning uses it to
deliberately reshape what a model attends to,

00:06:00.644 --> 00:06:05.039
removing reliance on spurious cues where ordinary
fine-tuning cannot.

00:06:05.119 --> 00:06:06.855
Mode connectivity, in short,

00:06:06.935 --> 00:06:13.600
is not merely a geometric curiosity but a practical
handle for building more robust and editable models.
